ies are something moulton  combination targeted therapies or  something we're going to see a lot more  off over the next few years because it  will really give us a improve duration  of response if you were targeting both  the driver mutation and its primary  resistance mechanism at the same time do  you think that as technologies evolve  that we will start to look at serial  monitoring for these patients as a  viable alternative to get ahead of  treating them and keeping them healthy  right and another great question I think  one of the drawbacks in doing serial  monitoring of patients with tissue is  that it's an invasive process every time  a patient has a navigational  bronchoscopy or a CT guided biopsy  performed that's one of the exciting  things about the emerging field of  liquid biopsies and plasma based testing  because we we can then start performing  serial testing perhaps even looking at  the at the load of mutation present in a  patient's bloodstream if their tumor is  shedding DNA and then probably determine  the development of a resistance mutation  we will see that before we can see it in  any tissue biopsy so I think that really  is going to be what we're doing the  other thing that's nice about that is  while ct-guided biopsy zin navigational  bronchoscopy usually are performed in  your larger community settings or in  your academic higher-tier medical  centers you don't see a lot of that in  the community setting or a patient may  have to travel a long distance to have a  biopsy I say everybody has a great  phlebotomist so I think the availability  of the liquid biopsy and plasma based  testing is really going to open up  serial monitoring and more testing in  the community setting because this can  be performed with a simple blood draw  rather than having to travel in many  instances to have a targeted biopsy by a  pulmonologist or a radiologist performed  so a question about if you if there is a  need to do a test and the lab did not  have the capability to perform that test  themselves in house what are some of the  questions that they can ask or some of  the data that they can look at to ensure  that the lab  they're looking to send their samples to  your as an accurate quality lab any  thoughts on that right that's a good  question is how do you how do you vet a  lab so to speak that you are sending out  testing too many of the lads the large  commercial reference labs are happy to  share their QA QC processes or tell you  how they validated a test which can be  exhaustive many of them have published  their data I would always shy away from  a lab that is hesitant to share what  tests they perform what method they use  or tell you how they validated a test I  think you know you want to look for a  lab that's not really hiding anything  you know a lab should be an open shop to  tell you how they perform a testing and  how they validate testing because if  you're comfortable with your methods I  don't think anyone is uncomfortable with  telling people about those processes and  it can be quite challenging because  there is a lot of direct marketing of  testing to mycologist and in many  instances many of these companies are  avoiding the pathology loop altogether  and directly marking to the oncologist  so it can be very challenging for a  provider to determine what is a good  accurate method to be used for their  patients versus what is a slick  marketing brochure that looks good but  may not be the best type of testing for  your patience so certainly a great  question okay I think we have time  probably for one or two more questions  so if you'd like to submit questions  please use your Q&amp;A button there's one  here for you do you have any thoughts on  recommending app tomers as an  alternative to antibodies this really  something that is far out of my area  especially so I'm not going to attempt  to show my ignorance about app tumors  and antibody testing by attempting to  answer it but I'm sure there's somebody  out there much better qualified than I  am I'm much more of a DNA driven person  than an antibody driven question so I'm  sorry for not being being able to answer  that for you  okay this time we don't have any more  questions so I would once again like to  thank dr. longshore for his wonderful  presentation and do you have any final  comments before we conclude no I just  thanks everybody for your participation  today and I hope you have found the the  comments useful thank you again to lab  roots and to roche diagnostics for  supporting this and many thanks to karen  for moderating the session today okay  reminder for listeners out there this is  an educational webinar and thus offers  free continuing education credits so  once we conclude the webinar please  click on the c/e button located in the  bottom left hand corner of your web page  and follow the process to obtain your  credits before we go I want to let  everyone know that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through October 7th of this year you  will receive an email from lab roots  letting you know when this webcast will  be available for replay please share  that announcement with your colleagues  who may have missed today's event thank  you so much for joining us we hope to  see you again soon and that concludes  our presentation for today
 

